import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Plugins: Fudge" description="The planned JSON-over-stdio plugin protocol for Fudge. Planned for v0.3.">
  <article class="page">
    <h1>Plugins</h1>
    <p class="lead">Planned for v0.3. This is a design, not a shipped feature, and nothing here runs today.</p>

    <h2>The idea</h2>
    <p>A plugin is a program, in any language, that exchanges JSON over stdin and stdout. Fudge's built-in features use the same provider interface, so plugins are meant to be first class.</p>

    <h2>Layout</h2>
    <p>Each plugin lives in its own folder with a manifest.</p>
<pre><code>~/.local/share/fudge/plugins/&lt;id&gt;/plugin.toml</code></pre>
<pre><code>id      = "hello"
keyword = "hi"
exec    = "python3 plugin.py"
version = "0.1.0"</code></pre>

    <h2>Protocol</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Fudge starts your program the first time its keyword is typed, and keeps it running.</li>
      <li>For each query, it writes one JSON line to stdin, such as <code>{`{"method":"query","params":{"text":"hi bob"}}`}</code>.</li>
      <li>You reply with one JSON line holding a list of results. Each has a title, a subtitle and an action: <code>open</code>, <code>copy</code>, <code>run</code> or <code>paste</code>.</li>
      <li>The host enforces timeouts, so a slow plugin cannot freeze the box.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Field names are not final.</p>

    <h2>Example</h2>
    <p>A draft Python plugin that replies with a greeting and copies it on Enter.</p>
<pre><code>#!/usr/bin/env python3
import json, sys

for line in sys.stdin:
    msg = json.loads(line)
    if msg.get("method") != "query":
        continue
    text = msg["params"]["text"].strip()
    name = text.removeprefix("hi").strip() or "world"
    reply = &#123;
        "results": [&#123;
            "title": f"Hello, &#123;name&#125;",
            "subtitle": "Press Enter to copy",
            "action": &#123;"type": "copy", "text": f"Hello, &#123;name&#125;"&#125;,
        &#125;]
    &#125;
    print(json.dumps(reply), flush=True)</code></pre>

    <h2>Registry</h2>
    <p>Planned as a git-backed registry: a GitHub repository with an <code>index.json</code> signed with minisign, verified before anything installs. Downloads happen only when you browse or install, as the <a href="../privacy/">privacy page</a> lists.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
